`mech bundle-web`
===============================================================================

`mech bundle-web` bundles a configured browser project into static web assets.

1. Usage
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

```sh
mech bundle-web examples/browser-dom-demo --out dist/browser-dom-demo
```

2. Required project config
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

`bundle-web` requires nonempty `run.paths` and `serve.paths` in the project config. The HTML shim and wasm package may be supplied through `serve.shim` and `serve.wasm` or through the `--shim` and `--wasm` command-line options.

Node.js 22 or newer must be available when publishing. Before writing bundle
files, the supplied WASM package compiles the retained root and imports using
its own language and host features, without activating a program or starting
input drivers. A package that cannot compile the source is rejected; native
compiler acceptance alone does not prove browser compatibility. Rebuild older
packages with `python scripts/build-wasm.py --profile browser` (or the selected
compute profile) to obtain this admission API. Custom packages may remain narrow
provided they support the actual source closure.

```mech
config := {
  serve: {
    paths: ["demo.mec"]
    shim: "index.html"
    wasm: "../../src/wasm/pkg"
  }
  run: {
    paths: ["demo.mec"]
  }
}
```

3. Output layout
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The bundle output includes:

```text
index.html
style.css
pkg/
source/
code/
html/
```

4. Static URL rules
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Static bundles should use relative browser URLs such as `./_mech/project.js`, `./pkg/mech_wasm.js`, and `./source/demo.mec`. Server-root URLs such as `/_mech/project.js` depend on `mech serve` and are rejected for static bundles.
